{**
 * c-popover - a button that opens a small panel beside it, with a title and a
 * sentence: what is worth knowing at this place and not worth a page.
 *
 * The panel is a native popover, opened by the button's popovertarget, so none
 * of its opening and closing is script of this project's: a click outside it or
 * Escape closes it, the focus goes back to the button, and the browser tells
 * assistive technology the button is expanded while it is open. It is drawn in
 * the top layer, without a z-index.
 *
 * It is a dialog named by its title - a piece of the page of its own that
 * somebody opened - so it is announced as what it is about. It comes straight
 * after the button in the page, which is where a screen reader reading on and
 * the Tab key both find it.
 *
 * It hangs under the button, or over it when asked to, by CSS anchor
 * positioning, and turns to the other side when there is no room; a browser that
 * cannot anchor is served by assets/anchor.ts, which reads data-side.
 *
 * @param string $id the panel's id, which has to be unique on the page
 * @param string $side below or above: where the panel hangs when there is room
 *}

{import 'button.latte'}

{define popover, string $id, string $label, string $title, string $text, string $side = 'below', ?string $testId = null}
    <div class="c-popover" style="anchor-name: --{$id}" data-testid="{$testId}">
        {include button, label: $label, variant: 'quiet', attributes: ['popovertarget' => $id]}

        <div
            id="{$id}"
            class="c-popover__panel"
            popover
            role="dialog"
            aria-labelledby="{$id}-title"
            data-side="{$side === 'above' ? 'above' : 'below'}"
            style="position-anchor: --{$id}"
        >
            <p id="{$id}-title" class="c-popover__title">{$title}</p>
            <p class="c-popover__text">{$text}</p>
        </div>
    </div>
{/define}
